WALL(1)                        P32W Unix                         WALL(1)

NAME
       wall - write to all users

SYNOPSIS
       wall [file]

DESCRIPTION
       wall reads a message (from file, or the standard input up to
       its end) and writes it to every terminal with a user logged
       in (/etc/utmp), yours included, after the bell and a header

              Broadcast message from ken (tty0) at 14:05 ...

       Only the first 2000 bytes are sent (wall says so if there
       were more).

       Terminals whose users have said mesg n are skipped, unless
       you are root: root's wall reaches everyone (as the
       warnings of shutdown(8) do). Control characters other than
       tab and bell are shown as ^X, and bytes over 127 as M- and
       the character.

EXAMPLES
       $ wall
       The disk is nearly full: please clean up your files.
       ^D

       # echo 'Going down at 18:00' | wall

FILES
       /etc/utmp      who is logged in where
       /bin/wall      set-group-id tty: mode 2755, owner root,
                      group tty

EXIT STATUS
       0; 1 if file or /etc/utmp cannot be read; 2 for a usage
       error. A terminal that cannot be written is skipped
       silently.

SEE ALSO
       write(1), mesg(1), who(1), shutdown(8)

BUGS
       wall opens the terminals first, with the group tty, then
       gives the group up (setgid(2) to your own) and reads file
       with your own rights; so a message typed slowly holds the
       terminals open meanwhile. It does not say whom it skipped.
